    'chapterTitle': 'Essay Writing',
    'body': 'Essay writing in WAEC/WASSCE tests your ability to communicate ideas clearly in written English. You are usually asked to write between 450 and 600 words on a chosen topic. The main types are formal letters, informal letters, articles, speeches, narrative essays, argumentative essays, expository essays and descriptive essays.\n\nA formal letter (for example, a letter to the Principal or the Commissioner) uses two addresses, a formal salutation like \'Dear Sir/Madam\', a clear heading, and ends with \'Yours faithfully\'. An informal letter (to a friend or relative) uses only the writer\'s address, an opening like \'Dear Toluwani\', and ends with \'Your loving friend\'. Articles and speeches need a strong title or greeting, a clear introduction, well-developed body paragraphs and a firm conclusion. Narrative essays tell a story with a setting, characters and a climax. Argumentative essays take a stand and defend it with reasons and evidence, while expository essays explain a topic and descriptive essays paint a picture using sensory details.\n\nGood essays share four qualities: relevant content, logical organisation, correct grammar and appropriate register. Always plan before writing, use paragraphs of 4-6 sentences, vary sentence length, and proofread for tense and spelling errors.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Formal letter task: \'Write a letter to the Chairman of Zetra Ltd applying for a vacation job.\' Use two addresses, formal tone, state qualifications, close with \'Yours faithfully, Oyedele Adebayo\'.\n2. Argumentative essay: \'Boarding schools are better than day schools.\' Take a side, give three strong reasons (discipline, study time, independence), refute one opposing view.\n3. Narrative essay: \'A day I will never forget.\' Set the scene, build tension, describe the climax, end with a reflection.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Read the question carefully and identify the essay type.\n- Plan an outline before writing.\n- Follow the required word count (450-600 words).\n- Formal letters need two addresses; informal letters need one.\n- Use \'Yours faithfully\' with \'Dear Sir\', and \'Yours sincerely\' with a named addressee.\n- Articles need a catchy title and by-line.\n- Speeches begin with vocatives such as \'The Principal, teachers, fellow students\'.\n- Narratives need setting, characters, conflict and resolution.\n- Argumentative essays must take a clear stand.\n- Expository essays explain how or why.\n- Use paragraphs of 4-6 sentences.\n- Maintain consistent tense.\n- Vary sentence structure.\n- Use linking words: however, moreover, therefore.\n- Avoid contractions in formal writing.\n- Proofread for spelling, punctuation and concord.\n- End with a strong conclusion that restates your position.',
