    'chapterTitle': 'Comprehension',
    'body': 'Comprehension tests how well you understand a written passage. In WAEC/WASSCE you read one or two passages and answer questions on meaning, implied meaning, tone, attitude, grammatical function and vocabulary in context. Success depends on active reading rather than skimming.\n\nStart by reading the passage quickly to get the general idea, then read again slowly, underlining key ideas and unfamiliar words. Read the questions before your second reading so you know what to look for. Literal questions ask what the passage directly says; inferential questions ask what it implies. Tone questions require you to detect the writer\'s feeling (angry, sarcastic, admiring, regretful), while attitude questions ask how the writer views the subject (critical, sympathetic, neutral). Vocabulary questions want a word or phrase that can replace the underlined word without changing the meaning; always check the part of speech. Grammatical-function questions ask what job a group of words does in the sentence, for example \'adjectival phrase qualifying the noun boy\'.\n\nAnswer in complete sentences unless told otherwise, and use your own words where possible. Do not copy long stretches from the passage. Keep answers short and direct.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Passage sentence: \'Oyedele frowned as he read the letter from Zetra.\' Question: What was Oyedele\'s attitude? Answer: He was displeased or worried about the letter\'s content.\n2. Underlined word \'meagre\' in context \'a meagre salary\'. Choose the closest meaning: small/inadequate.\n3. Grammatical function of \'who arrived late\' in \'The student who arrived late was punished.\' Answer: Adjectival clause qualifying \'student\'.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Read the passage twice.\n- Skim first, then read carefully.\n- Read the questions before the second reading.\n- Underline key ideas.\n- Distinguish literal from inferential questions.\n- Tone = writer\'s feeling; attitude = writer\'s view.\n- Match vocabulary answers by part of speech.\n- Use your own words where possible.\n- Answer in complete sentences.\n- Keep answers short and precise.\n- Do not copy long chunks.\n- Watch for signal words: however, therefore, although.\n- Note figures of speech in the passage.\n- Identify the main idea of each paragraph.\n- Check tense before answering.\n- Never introduce outside information.\n- Proofread before submitting.',
