    'chapterTitle': 'Summary Writing',
    'body': 'Summary writing tests your ability to identify main ideas in a passage and restate them briefly in your own words. In WAEC/WASSCE you are usually asked for a fixed number of points (often three or four), each in one sentence of not more than a stated number of words.\n\nBegin by reading the passage twice for full understanding. Then read the specific instruction carefully; it will tell you what to look for, e.g. \'three ways the government can reduce unemployment\' or \'four reasons Oyedele decided to join Zetra\'. Underline every sentence that directly answers the instruction and ignore examples, illustrations and repetitions. Group related ideas together so you do not repeat yourself. Convert each idea into a single, clear sentence using your own words as much as possible. Keep the tense consistent with the passage.\n\nAvoid direct quotation, personal opinion, figurative language and unnecessary linking words. Write in simple, complete sentences. Number your points as instructed. Count your words and stay within the limit.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Instruction: \'In one sentence of not more than 15 words, state why Toluwani rejected the offer.\' Answer: \'Toluwani rejected the offer because the salary was too low for her responsibilities.\'\n2. Instruction: \'In three sentences, one for each, list three benefits of exercise mentioned in the passage.\' Write three separate sentences beginning with different structures.\n3. Passage mentions \'Zetra trains staff, pays well, and promotes hard workers.\' Summarise as: \'Zetra offers training, good pay and promotion opportunities.\'\n\nSummary Points:\n- Read the passage twice.\n- Read the instruction carefully.\n- Note the exact number of points required.\n- Note the word limit per point.\n- Underline relevant ideas only.\n- Ignore examples and repetitions.\n- Use your own words where possible.\n- Write complete sentences.\n- Avoid direct quotation.\n- Do not add personal opinion.\n- Do not use figures of speech.\n- Keep tense consistent.\n- Number your points as directed.\n- Group related ideas.\n- Avoid connectives like \'firstly\' inside the sentence.\n- Count your words.\n- Proofread for grammar and concord.',
