    'chapterTitle': 'Tenses',
    'body': 'Tense shows the time of an action or state. English has three main times (past, present, future) and each has four aspects (simple, continuous, perfect, perfect continuous), giving twelve tenses.\n\nSimple tenses state a fact or habit: \'Oyedele writes reports\' (present simple), \'Oyedele wrote a report\' (past simple), \'Oyedele will write a report\' (future simple). Continuous tenses show an action in progress and use \'be + verb-ing\': \'Toluwani is studying\', \'Toluwani was studying\', \'Toluwani will be studying\'. Perfect tenses link two times and use \'have + past participle\': \'She has finished\' (present perfect, action completed with present relevance), \'She had finished before I arrived\' (past perfect, one past action before another), \'She will have finished by noon\' (future perfect). Perfect continuous tenses stress the duration of an ongoing action and use \'have been + verb-ing\': \'He has been working at Zetra for five years.\'\n\nCommon errors include mixing tenses within one sentence, using the past tense after \'did\' (\'did you went?\' should be \'did you go?\'), and confusing \'since\' (a point in time) with \'for\' (a length of time). Time markers help you choose the right tense: \'yesterday\' takes past simple, \'already\' and \'just\' take present perfect, \'while\' takes past continuous.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. \'Toluwani ____ (read) when the light went off.\' Answer: was reading (past continuous with past simple).\n2. \'Oyedele ____ (work) at Zetra since 2020.\' Answer: has worked / has been working (present perfect / perfect continuous with \'since\').\n3. \'By this time tomorrow, we ____ (finish) the project.\' Answer: will have finished (future perfect).\n\nSummary Points:\n- English has 12 tenses: 3 times x 4 aspects.\n- Simple tense = fact or habit.\n- Continuous = action in progress (be + -ing).\n- Perfect = completed with link to another time (have + V3).\n- Perfect continuous = duration of ongoing action (have been + -ing).\n- \'Since\' + point in time; \'for\' + length of time.\n- \'Yesterday\', \'ago\', \'last week\' need past simple.\n- \'Already\', \'just\', \'ever\', \'never\' need present perfect.\n- Use past continuous for background action.\n- After \'did/does/do\' use base form.\n- Keep tense consistent within a narrative.\n- Reported speech usually shifts one tense back.\n- Future can also be shown with \'be going to\'.\n- Present simple can express future in timetables.\n- Watch state verbs (know, love) that avoid continuous form.\n- Proofread for tense agreement.',
