    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Concord',
    'body': 'Concord (agreement) is the grammatical rule that a verb must agree with its subject in number and person. A singular subject takes a singular verb; a plural subject takes a plural verb. Concord is tested heavily in WAEC/WASSCE lexis and structure.\n\nThe main rules are: (1) Singular subjects joined by \'and\' usually take a plural verb: \'Oyedele and Toluwani are here.\' Exception: when the two nouns refer to one thing or person, use a singular verb: \'Bread and butter is my breakfast.\' (2) Subjects joined by \'or\', \'either...or\', \'neither...nor\' take a verb agreeing with the nearer subject: \'Neither the manager nor the clerks were present.\' (3) Collective nouns (team, family, committee, government, staff) take a singular verb when acting as one unit and a plural verb when members act individually: \'The Zetra staff is loyal\' vs \'The staff are quarrelling among themselves.\' (4) Indefinite pronouns like \'everyone, anybody, each, either, neither, none, nobody\' are singular: \'Everyone has arrived.\' (5) Nouns plural in form but singular in meaning (news, mathematics, physics, measles) take singular verbs. (6) A phrase between subject and verb does not affect agreement: \'The box of pens is on the table.\' (7) \'A number of\' is plural; \'the number of\' is singular.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. \'One of the boys ____ (has/have) come.\' Answer: has (subject is \'one\').\n2. \'Neither Oyedele nor his friends ____ (was/were) at Zetra.\' Answer: were (agrees with \'friends\', the nearer subject).\n3. \'The news ____ (is/are) shocking.\' Answer: is (\'news\' is singular).\n\nSummary Points:\n- Subject and verb must agree in number.\n- \'And\' joining subjects usually takes plural verb.\n- Two nouns referring to one thing take singular verb.\n- \'Or/nor\' takes verb agreeing with nearer subject.\n- Collective nouns can be singular or plural by sense.\n- Indefinite pronouns (each, every, everyone) are singular.\n- \'None\' can be singular or plural depending on context.\n- Nouns like news, mathematics, physics are singular.\n- Nouns like scissors, trousers, pliers are plural.\n- Intervening phrases do not change subject.\n- \'A number of\' = plural; \'the number of\' = singular.\n- \'Many a\' + singular noun + singular verb.\n- Titles of books are singular.\n- Distances, sums of money, time periods are singular.\n- Relative pronoun verb agrees with antecedent.\n- Proofread for hidden subjects.',
