    'chapterTitle': 'Parts of Speech',
    'body': 'The parts of speech are the eight (some grammarians say nine) categories into which every English word can be placed according to its function in a sentence. They are noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, conjunction and interjection; the article is sometimes added as the ninth.\n\nA noun names a person, place, thing or idea (Oyedele, Lagos, Zetra, honesty). Nouns can be common, proper, abstract, concrete, countable or uncountable. A pronoun replaces a noun (he, she, it, they, who, myself). A verb expresses action or state (write, run, is, seem); it may be transitive, intransitive, or a linking verb. An adjective describes a noun (tall, honest, three, this). An adverb modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, usually answering how, when, where or to what extent (quickly, yesterday, here, very). A preposition shows relationship between a noun/pronoun and another word (in, on, at, between, because of). A conjunction joins words or clauses (and, but, or, because, although). An interjection expresses sudden emotion (Oh!, Alas!, Wow!). The same word can belong to different classes depending on use: \'water\' can be a noun (\'drink water\') or a verb (\'water the plants\').\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. In \'Toluwani quickly wrote a beautiful letter to Zetra\', identify parts of speech: Toluwani (noun), quickly (adverb), wrote (verb), a (article), beautiful (adjective), letter (noun), to (preposition), Zetra (noun).\n2. \'She sings well but he dances better.\' \'But\' is a conjunction; \'well\' and \'better\' are adverbs.\n3. \'Oh! The bag is heavy.\' \'Oh\' is an interjection; \'heavy\' is an adjective describing \'bag\'.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Eight main parts of speech, plus article.\n- Noun: names person, place, thing, idea.\n- Pronoun: replaces noun.\n- Verb: action or state.\n- Adjective: qualifies noun.\n- Adverb: modifies verb, adjective or adverb.\n- Preposition: shows relationship.\n- Conjunction: joins words/clauses.\n- Interjection: sudden emotion.\n- Articles: a, an, the.\n- Same word can be different parts of speech.\n- Look at function, not the word itself.\n- Proper nouns begin with capital letters.\n- Abstract nouns name ideas or feelings.\n- Transitive verbs take an object.\n- Intransitive verbs do not take an object.\n- Linking verbs join subject to complement.\n- Coordinating conjunctions: FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so).\n- Subordinating conjunctions introduce dependent clauses.',
