    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Figures of Speech',
    'body': 'A figure of speech is a word or expression used in a non-literal way to create a particular effect. WAEC/WASSCE focuses on the most common ones: simile, metaphor, personification, irony, hyperbole, onomatopoeia, oxymoron, alliteration, apostrophe and euphemism.\n\nA simile compares two different things using \'like\' or \'as\': \'Oyedele runs like a cheetah.\' A metaphor makes the same comparison without \'like\' or \'as\': \'Toluwani is a shining star at Zetra.\' Personification gives human qualities to non-human things: \'The wind whispered through the trees.\' Irony says the opposite of what is meant, or shows a mismatch between expectation and outcome: telling a lazy student \'You are so hardworking.\' Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration for effect: \'I have told you a million times.\' Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds: buzz, hiss, splash, thud. Oxymoron joins contradictory terms: \'bitter sweet\', \'deafening silence\'. Alliteration repeats the same initial consonant sound: \'Toluwani\'s tiny toes tapped.\' Apostrophe addresses an absent person or an idea as if present: \'O Death, where is thy sting?\' Euphemism replaces a harsh expression with a mild one: \'passed away\' for \'died\'.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. \'Zetra\'s new building touches the sky.\' Figure: hyperbole (exaggeration).\n2. \'The bees buzzed angrily around Oyedele.\' Figures: onomatopoeia (buzzed) and personification (angrily).\n3. \'Her smile was a ray of sunshine.\' Figure: metaphor.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Simile: comparison with like/as.\n- Metaphor: direct comparison without like/as.\n- Personification: human traits to non-humans.\n- Irony: opposite of literal meaning.\n- Hyperbole: exaggeration.\n- Onomatopoeia: sound imitation.\n- Oxymoron: contradictory pair.\n- Alliteration: repeated initial consonant.\n- Assonance: repeated vowel sound.\n- Apostrophe: address to absent/abstract entity.\n- Euphemism: mild for harsh.\n- Metonymy: naming by association (\'the Crown\' for the king).\n- Synecdoche: part for whole (\'wheels\' for car).\n- Paradox: seemingly contradictory but true.\n- Litotes: understatement using negatives.\n- Figures create imagery and emphasis.\n- Identify by effect, not by the word alone.',
