    'chapterTitle': 'Phrasal Verbs and Idioms',
    'body': 'A phrasal verb is a verb combined with a preposition or adverb (or both) whose meaning is different from the sum of the individual words. An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning cannot be guessed from the individual words. Both are common in WAEC/WASSCE lexis and structure and in oral tests.\n\nExamples of phrasal verbs: \'give up\' (stop trying), \'put up with\' (tolerate), \'look into\' (investigate), \'call off\' (cancel), \'break down\' (stop functioning or lose emotional control), \'come across\' (find by chance), \'run out of\' (exhaust supply), \'take after\' (resemble a relative). Some phrasal verbs are separable: \'Oyedele turned the offer down\' or \'Oyedele turned down the offer.\' Others are inseparable: \'She takes after her mother\' (not \'takes her mother after\').\n\nExamples of idioms: \'bite the bullet\' (endure something painful bravely), \'kick the bucket\' (die), \'a piece of cake\' (very easy), \'in hot water\' (in trouble), \'let the cat out of the bag\' (reveal a secret), \'burn the midnight oil\' (study late into the night), \'once in a blue moon\' (very rarely), \'hit the nail on the head\' (say exactly the right thing).\n\nLearn them in context by reading widely and by writing example sentences. Never translate them word-for-word into your first language.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. \'Zetra decided to call off the meeting.\' \'Call off\' means cancel.\n2. \'Toluwani burned the midnight oil to prepare for WAEC.\' Idiom = studied very late.\n3. \'Oyedele takes after his father in appearance.\' \'Takes after\' means resembles.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Phrasal verb = verb + preposition/adverb with new meaning.\n- Idiom = fixed expression with figurative meaning.\n- Do not translate literally.\n- Some phrasal verbs are separable; some are not.\n- Pronoun objects go between verb and particle: \'turn it down\'.\n- Learn in context, not in isolation.\n- Common: give up, put up with, look into, call off.\n- Common idioms: piece of cake, hot water, blue moon.\n- Read newspapers, novels and speeches for exposure.\n- Note register: idioms are usually informal.\n- Avoid mixing idioms.\n- Do not overuse idioms in formal writing.\n- Check tense of the main verb.\n- Some phrasal verbs have multiple meanings.\n- A dictionary of phrasal verbs is a good study tool.\n- Practise by rewriting sentences using idioms.\n- Master 5-10 new expressions per week.',
