    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Reported/Indirect Speech',
    'body': 'Reported speech (indirect speech) is the way we report what someone said without quoting the exact words. Direct speech uses quotation marks and the speaker\'s exact words: Oyedele said, \'I am tired.\' Indirect speech drops the quotation marks and often changes tense, pronouns and time/place words: Oyedele said that he was tired.\n\nMain rules of conversion: (1) If the reporting verb is in the past tense (said, told, asked), the tense of the reported verb usually shifts one step back: present simple > past simple; present continuous > past continuous; present perfect and past simple > past perfect; will > would; can > could; may > might; must > had to. (2) Pronouns change to fit the new speaker: \'I\' > \'he/she\'; \'we\' > \'they\'; \'you\' > \'I/we/they\' depending on context. (3) Time and place words change: now > then; today > that day; tomorrow > the next day; yesterday > the previous day; here > there; this > that. (4) Questions become statements introduced by \'if/whether\' (yes/no questions) or by the question word (wh-questions), and word order becomes normal statement order. (5) Commands and requests use \'told/asked + object + to-infinitive\'.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Direct: Toluwani said, \'I am writing my WAEC exam now.\' Indirect: Toluwani said that she was writing her WAEC exam then.\n2. Direct: The manager asked, \'Do you work at Zetra?\' Indirect: The manager asked whether I worked at Zetra.\n3. Direct: Oyedele said to me, \'Close the door.\' Indirect: Oyedele told me to close the door.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Direct speech uses quotation marks.\n- Indirect speech reports the message.\n- Reporting verb in past shifts reported tense one step back.\n- Present > past; past > past perfect.\n- Will > would; can > could; may > might.\n- Modals \'would, could, might, should, ought to, must (obligation)\' do not usually change.\n- Change pronouns to fit context.\n- Change time/place words.\n- Yes/no questions use \'if/whether\'.\n- Wh-questions keep the question word.\n- Change question order to statement order.\n- Drop question marks in reported questions.\n- Commands use \'told + object + to + verb\'.\n- Negative commands: \'not to + verb\'.\n- Use \'said to\' > \'told\' when object is present.\n- Universal truths remain in present tense.\n- Punctuation is critical in direct speech.',
