    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Question Tags',
    'body': 'A question tag is a short question added to the end of a statement to seek confirmation or agreement. It is very common in spoken and written English and features regularly in WAEC/WASSCE objective tests.\n\nThe basic rule is: positive statement > negative tag; negative statement > positive tag. The tag uses the auxiliary verb of the main clause (or \'do/does/did\' if there is none) plus the pronoun form of the subject. Examples: \'Oyedele is a teacher, isn\'t he?\' / \'Toluwani doesn\'t work at Zetra, does she?\' / \'They will come, won\'t they?\'\n\nSpecial cases: (1) \'I am\' takes \'aren\'t I?\' as its tag: \'I am your friend, aren\'t I?\' (2) Imperatives take \'will you?\' or \'won\'t you?\': \'Close the door, will you?\' (3) \'Let us\' (suggestion) takes \'shall we?\': \'Let us go, shall we?\' (4) \'Let me/him\' (permission) takes \'will you?\'. (5) Sentences with negative words like \'never, hardly, seldom, no one, nothing\' are treated as negative and take a positive tag: \'Nobody came, did they?\' (6) With \'everyone, someone, nobody\', use \'they\' in the tag. (7) \'This/that\' becomes \'it\' in the tag; \'these/those\' becomes \'they\'.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Oyedele has finished the report, ____? Answer: hasn\'t he.\n2. Nothing was left in the office, ____? Answer: was there / was it (\'nothing\' is negative, so positive tag).\n3. Let us pray, ____? Answer: shall we.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Positive statement + negative tag.\n- Negative statement + positive tag.\n- Use the same auxiliary as the main verb.\n- If no auxiliary, use do/does/did.\n- \'I am\' > \'aren\'t I\'.\n- Imperatives > \'will you / won\'t you\'.\n- \'Let us\' (suggestion) > \'shall we\'.\n- \'Let me/him\' (permission) > \'will you\'.\n- Negative words (never, hardly, no one) count as negative.\n- \'Everybody/someone/nobody\' > \'they\' in tag.\n- \'This/that\' > \'it\'; \'these/those\' > \'they\'.\n- Contractions are used in the tag: isn\'t, don\'t.\n- Tag agrees with subject in person and number.\n- Punctuation: comma before tag, question mark after.\n- Intonation rises for genuine question, falls for confirmation.\n- Common mistake: wrong auxiliary.\n- Practise by converting statements to tag questions.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
