    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Clause Analysis',
    'body': 'A clause is a group of words containing a subject and a finite verb. Clauses are either independent (main) or dependent (subordinate). Subordinate clauses are classified by function into noun clauses, adjectival (relative) clauses and adverbial clauses.\n\nA noun clause does the work of a noun and can be subject, object, complement or appositive. It often begins with \'that, what, whether, if, who, why, how\'. Example: \'That Oyedele passed the exam pleased his parents.\' (\'That Oyedele passed the exam\' is the subject.) Another: \'Toluwani knows what she wants.\' (\'What she wants\' is object of \'knows\'.)\n\nAn adjectival clause (relative clause) qualifies a noun or pronoun and usually begins with a relative pronoun (who, whom, whose, which, that) or a relative adverb (where, when, why). Example: \'The manager who leads Zetra is efficient.\' (\'Who leads Zetra\' qualifies \'manager\'.)\n\nAn adverbial clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb and answers when, where, why, how, to what extent, on what condition or in spite of what. Subclasses include time (when, while, before), place (where, wherever), reason (because, since), purpose (so that, in order that), result (so...that), condition (if, unless), concession (although, though), comparison (as, than) and manner (as, as if). Example: \'Toluwani succeeded because she worked hard.\' (adverbial clause of reason).\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. \'I know that Oyedele works at Zetra.\' The clause \'that Oyedele works at Zetra\' is a noun clause, object of \'know\'.\n2. \'The book which Toluwani bought is interesting.\' \'Which Toluwani bought\' is an adjectival clause qualifying \'book\'.\n3. \'When the bell rang, we left the class.\' \'When the bell rang\' is an adverbial clause of time modifying \'left\'.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Clause = subject + finite verb.\n- Two main types: main (independent) and subordinate (dependent).\n- Subordinate clauses: noun, adjectival, adverbial.\n- Noun clause = does work of a noun.\n- Noun clause often starts with that, what, whether, if.\n- Adjectival clause = qualifies noun/pronoun.\n- Relative pronouns: who, whom, whose, which, that.\n- Relative adverbs: where, when, why.\n- Adverbial clause = modifies verb/adjective/adverb.\n- Adverbial subclasses: time, place, reason, purpose, result, condition, concession, comparison, manner.\n- Identify function, not the introducing word alone.\n- A clause differs from a phrase (phrase has no finite verb).\n- Test noun clause by replacing with \'it\' or \'something\'.\n- Test adjectival clause by asking \'which one?\'.\n- Test adverbial clause by asking when/why/how.\n- Multiple clauses can be nested.\n- Practise by underlining each clause and labelling it.',
