    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Synonyms, Antonyms and Homophones',
    'body': 'Vocabulary questions in WAEC/WASSCE test three related skills: choosing synonyms (words with similar meaning), antonyms (words with opposite meaning) and distinguishing homophones (words that sound alike but differ in meaning and spelling).\n\nA synonym is a word that can replace another with little change in meaning. For example, \'big\' and \'large\', \'happy\' and \'cheerful\', \'brave\' and \'courageous\'. However, no two words are perfectly identical; check the context, register and part of speech before choosing. \'Slim\' and \'skinny\' both mean thin, but \'slim\' is complimentary while \'skinny\' is negative.\n\nAn antonym is a word of opposite meaning: \'accept\' vs \'reject\', \'ancient\' vs \'modern\', \'generous\' vs \'stingy\'. Some words have several antonyms depending on context; \'hard\' can be opposite to \'soft\' (texture) or \'easy\' (difficulty).\n\nHomophones share pronunciation but differ in spelling and meaning: \'their/there/they\'re\'; \'to/too/two\'; \'principal/principle\'; \'stationary/stationery\'; \'flour/flower\'; \'peace/piece\'; \'weather/whether\'; \'complement/compliment\'. Errors with homophones are common in essays and lower marks quickly.\n\nStrategy: read the sentence carefully, identify the part of speech, and choose the option that fits both meaning and grammar. If two options seem correct, pick the one nearer in register (formal/informal) to the original.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Synonym for \'diligent\' in \'Toluwani is a diligent student.\' Answer: hardworking / industrious.\n2. Antonym of \'generous\' in \'Oyedele is a generous man.\' Answer: stingy / mean.\n3. Choose the correct homophone: \'The ____ (principal/principle) of Zetra Academy welcomed us.\' Answer: principal (head of school).\n\nSummary Points:\n- Synonym = similar meaning.\n- Antonym = opposite meaning.\n- Homophone = same sound, different spelling/meaning.\n- Check part of speech before choosing.\n- Consider register (formal/informal).\n- No two synonyms are perfectly identical.\n- Some words have context-dependent antonyms.\n- Common homophones: their/there/they\'re, to/too/two.\n- Principal (head) vs principle (rule).\n- Stationary (still) vs stationery (writing materials).\n- Complement (complete) vs compliment (praise).\n- Peace (calm) vs piece (part).\n- Site (location) vs sight (view) vs cite (quote).\n- Weather (climate) vs whether (if).\n- Read widely to build vocabulary.\n- Use a thesaurus with care.\n- Practise past-question vocabulary sections regularly.\n- Keep a personal vocabulary notebook.',
