    'chapterTitle': 'Word Formation and Sentence Structure',
    'body': 'Word formation is the process by which new words are built in English. The main processes are affixation (adding prefixes or suffixes), conversion (changing part of speech without changing form), compounding (joining two words), and blending or clipping. Prefixes usually change meaning: \'un-\', \'dis-\', \'in-\', \'mis-\', \'re-\'. Suffixes usually change the part of speech: \'-tion\', \'-ment\', \'-ness\', \'-ity\' form nouns; \'-ise\', \'-ify\', \'-en\' form verbs; \'-ful\', \'-less\', \'-ous\', \'-able\' form adjectives; \'-ly\' forms adverbs. Examples: \'happy\' (adj) > \'happiness\' (noun) > \'happily\' (adv); \'act\' (verb) > \'action\' (noun) > \'active\' (adj) > \'activate\' (verb).\n\nSentence structure refers to how words combine into meaningful units. English sentences are classified in two ways. By purpose: declarative (states), interrogative (asks), imperative (commands) and exclamatory (expresses strong feeling). By structure: simple (one main clause), compound (two or more main clauses joined by coordinating conjunctions), complex (one main clause plus at least one subordinate clause), and compound-complex (two or more main clauses plus at least one subordinate). Example of complex: \'Because Oyedele studied hard, he passed the WAEC exam.\'\n\nA good sentence has clarity, unity and correctness. Avoid run-on sentences, sentence fragments, dangling modifiers and ambiguous references.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Form the noun from \'decide\': decision. Adjective: decisive. Adverb: decisively.\n2. Combine into a compound sentence: \'Toluwani studied hard. She passed.\' > \'Toluwani studied hard, so she passed.\'\n3. Identify the structure: \'When Zetra opened, Oyedele applied, and he was hired.\' > Compound-complex.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Word formation: affixation, conversion, compounding, blending, clipping.\n- Prefixes change meaning.\n- Suffixes change part of speech.\n- Common noun suffixes: -tion, -ment, -ness, -ity, -ance.\n- Common verb suffixes: -ise, -ify, -en, -ate.\n- Common adjective suffixes: -ful, -less, -ous, -able, -ive.\n- Adverb suffix: -ly.\n- Sentence purposes: declarative, interrogative, imperative, exclamatory.\n- Sentence structures: simple, compound, complex, compound-complex.\n- Coordinating conjunctions: FANBOYS.\n- Subordinating conjunctions introduce dependent clauses.\n- Avoid run-on sentences.\n- Avoid sentence fragments.\n- Watch for dangling modifiers.\n- Ensure clear pronoun reference.\n- Vary sentence length for style.\n- Use punctuation correctly to signal structure.',
