    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Vowel Sounds',
    'body': 'English has about 20 vowel sounds, more than the five vowel letters suggest. They are grouped into monophthongs (pure single vowels) and diphthongs (glides from one vowel position to another). WAEC/WASSCE oral English tests your ability to identify these sounds using phonetic symbols.\n\nMonophthongs are further divided into short vowels: /I/ as in \'sit\', /e/ as in \'bed\', /ae/ as in \'cat\', /ʌ/ as in \'cup\', /ɒ/ as in \'hot\', /ʊ/ as in \'put\', /ə/ (schwa) as in the first syllable of \'about\'; and long vowels: /i:/ as in \'seat\', /ɜ:/ as in \'bird\', /ɑ:/ as in \'car\', /ɔ:/ as in \'call\', /u:/ as in \'food\'. The colon marks length.\n\nDiphthongs are eight in number: /eɪ/ as in \'day\', /aɪ/ as in \'my\', /ɔɪ/ as in \'boy\', /əʊ/ as in \'go\', /aʊ/ as in \'now\', /ɪə/ as in \'ear\', /eə/ as in \'air\', /ʊə/ as in \'poor\'. Each glides from a starting vowel to an ending vowel within one syllable.\n\nTo master vowel sounds, listen to standard British or Nigerian broadcast English, use a good phonetic dictionary, and practise minimal pairs (words differing in only one sound), for example \'ship\' /I/ vs \'sheep\' /i:/, \'full\' /ʊ/ vs \'fool\' /u:/.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Identify the vowel sound in \'Oyedele\'. The stressed syllable \'de\' contains /e/, and the first \'O\' is roughly /əʊ/.\n2. Which word contains a diphthong: \'sit\', \'boy\', \'cup\'? Answer: \'boy\' contains /ɔɪ/.\n3. Choose the word with /i:/: \'bit\', \'beat\', \'bet\'. Answer: \'beat\'.\n\nSummary Points:\n- English has about 20 vowel sounds.\n- Monophthongs are pure single vowels.\n- Diphthongs glide from one position to another.\n- Short monophthongs: /I, e, ae, ʌ, ɒ, ʊ, ə/.\n- Long monophthongs: /i:, ɜ:, ɑ:, ɔ:, u:/.\n- The colon marks length.\n- Eight diphthongs: /eɪ, aɪ, ɔɪ, əʊ, aʊ, ɪə, eə, ʊə/.\n- Schwa /ə/ is the most common English vowel.\n- Practise minimal pairs (ship/sheep).\n- Vowel length affects meaning.\n- Use a phonetic dictionary.\n- Spelling does not always match sound.\n- Listen to standard broadcast English.\n- Record yourself and compare.\n- Learn IPA symbols.\n- Focus on sounds not present in your first language.\n- Test yourself with past oral questions.',
