    'chapterTitle': 'Consonant Sounds',
    'body': 'English has 24 consonant sounds classified by three criteria: place of articulation (where in the mouth), manner of articulation (how the air is obstructed) and voicing (whether the vocal cords vibrate).\n\nBy place, consonants are bilabial (/p, b, m, w/), labiodental (/f, v/), dental (/θ, ð/ as in \'think\' and \'this\'), alveolar (/t, d, s, z, n, l, r/), post-alveolar (/ʃ, ʒ, tʃ, dʒ/ as in \'ship, measure, church, judge\'), palatal (/j/ as in \'yes\'), velar (/k, g, ŋ/ as in \'sing\') and glottal (/h/).\n\nBy manner: plosives (/p, b, t, d, k, g/) involve complete blockage then release; fricatives (/f, v, θ, ð, s, z, ʃ, ʒ, h/) involve friction; affricates (/tʃ, dʒ/) combine plosive and fricative; nasals (/m, n, ŋ/) release air through the nose; laterals (/l/) release air along the sides of the tongue; approximants (/w, j, r/) narrow the mouth without friction.\n\nBy voicing: voiceless consonants have no vocal-cord vibration (/p, t, k, f, s, ʃ, θ, tʃ, h/); voiced consonants do (/b, d, g, v, z, ʒ, ð, dʒ, m, n, ŋ, l, r, w, j/). Voiced/voiceless pairs are common: /p-b/, /t-d/, /k-g/, /f-v/, /s-z/, /ʃ-ʒ/, /θ-ð/, /tʃ-dʒ/. Nigerian learners often struggle with /θ/ and /ð/ (\'think\', \'this\'), replacing them with /t/ or /d/.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. The initial sound in \'Toluwani\' is /t/ (voiceless alveolar plosive).\n2. \'Zetra\' begins with /z/ (voiced alveolar fricative).\n3. \'Oyedele\' contains /j/ (palatal approximant) at the start of the second syllable \'ye\'.\n\nSummary Points:\n- English has 24 consonant sounds.\n- Classified by place, manner and voicing.\n- Places: bilabial, labiodental, dental, alveolar, post-alveolar, palatal, velar, glottal.\n- Manners: plosive, fricative, affricate, nasal, lateral, approximant.\n- Voicing: voiced vs voiceless.\n- Voiced/voiceless pairs: p-b, t-d, k-g, f-v, s-z, ʃ-ʒ, θ-ð, tʃ-dʒ.\n- /θ/ and /ð/ are dental fricatives.\n- Nigerian learners often replace /θ, ð/ with /t, d/.\n- /ŋ/ is the sound at the end of \'sing\'.\n- Silent letters exist (knee, write, hour).\n- \'Ch\' can be /tʃ/, /k/ or /ʃ/ depending on origin.\n- Practise minimal pairs (pin/bin, fat/vat).\n- Learn IPA symbols.\n- Listen to natural English speech.\n- Record and self-correct.\n- Focus on sounds foreign to your first language.\n- Use past oral English questions for practice.',
