    'subject': 'English',
    'chapterTitle': 'Stress and Intonation',
    'body': 'Stress is the extra force given to a syllable within a word, or to a word within a sentence. Intonation is the melody or rise and fall of the voice across an utterance. Both carry meaning in English and are frequently tested in WAEC/WASSCE oral English.\n\nWord stress: every English word of more than one syllable has one main stressed syllable, marked in dictionaries by a small vertical stroke. In \'photograph\' the stress is on the first syllable (PHO-to-graph); in \'photography\' it moves to the second (pho-TO-gra-phy); in \'photographic\' it moves to the third (pho-to-GRA-phic). Changing stress can change part of speech: RE-cord (noun) vs re-CORD (verb); PRE-sent (noun/adj) vs pre-SENT (verb); CON-duct (noun) vs con-DUCT (verb).\n\nSentence stress: content words (nouns, main verbs, adjectives, adverbs, question words) are usually stressed, while function words (articles, auxiliaries, prepositions, pronouns, conjunctions) are usually unstressed and often reduced to schwa. Emphatic stress shifts to any word the speaker wants to highlight: \'OYEDELE gave the book to Toluwani\' (not someone else); \'Oyedele GAVE the book to Toluwani\' (did not lend it).\n\nIntonation has two main patterns. Falling intonation is used for statements, commands, wh-questions and completed thought: \'She works at Zetra.\'↓ Rising intonation is used for yes/no questions, polite requests, and incomplete lists: \'Is Oyedele home?\'↑ A fall-rise can express uncertainty or contrast.\n\nWorked Examples:\n1. Mark stress: \'necessary\' > NEC-es-sa-ry (first syllable). \'necessity\' > ne-CES-si-ty (second).\n2. Which is stressed differently as noun vs verb? \'export\': EX-port (noun) / ex-PORT (verb).\n3. \'Are you coming to Zetra tomorrow?\' takes rising intonation on \'tomorrow\'.\n\nSummary Points:\n- Stress = extra force on a syllable or word.\n- Every polysyllabic word has one main stress.\n- Stress can shift with suffixes.\n- Same spelling can differ in stress for noun vs verb.\n- Content words are usually stressed.\n- Function words are usually unstressed.\n- Unstressed vowels often become schwa /ə/.\n- Emphatic stress highlights a chosen word.\n- Intonation = rise and fall of the voice.\n- Falling tone: statements, commands, wh-questions.\n- Rising tone: yes/no questions, polite requests, incomplete lists.\n- Fall-rise tone: uncertainty or contrast.\n- Intonation can change meaning without changing words.\n- Question tags seeking real information use rising tone.\n- Question tags seeking confirmation use falling tone.\n- Listen to native or standard Nigerian broadcast English.\n- Mark stress with capitals or a stroke when practising.\n- Read poems and dialogues aloud.\n- Use a phonetic dictionary that marks stress.\n- Practise past oral English stress questions.',
